### 4. Required and conditional routes

#### 4.1 Required route — the primary path
Every paying user must be able to walk the full five-stage sequence for one subject. This is the only required route for version one.

#### 4.2 Conditional route — “Someone I love”
Triggered by choosing “Someone I love” in Stage 1.  
- **User problem solved:** A person helping another needs the same structure without mixing up their own health information with the loved one’s.  
- **Why needed:** Many people seek clarity for a parent, partner or friend. Forcing them to answer as if it were about themselves would create confusion and harm trust.  
- **Contributes to:** All outputs, with appropriate labelling (e.g., “Prepared for [relationship/nickname]”).  
- **Required or conditional:** Conditional — only shown when the user indicates they are not the subject.  
- **When user does not know:** They can still continue; the subject’s details will simply be minimal.  
- **Concerns:** User (as helper) and the person they love.  
- **Privacy issue:** The application must keep the helper’s own data (if they later use Clarify for themselves) entirely separate from the loved one’s profile.  
- **Unnecessary burden:** Asking for full identity details of the loved one too early. We only ask for a first name or nickname and relationship initially.

#### 4.3 Conditional route — “Start a new clarification”
After completing one subject, a user may create an additional profile for another person (including themselves in a different context). Each profile is stored independently. No limit is placed on the number of profiles in version one. The user can switch between profiles without deleting previous work.

