### 5. Information model

The model is designed to separate personally identifying information (PII) from health, lifestyle and family information. The user consciously decides what to combine only when generating a shareable output.

Every field is listed below with the required rationale block.

#### 5.1 Subject identity record (PII — kept separate)
These fields are stored in an `identity` object that is never automatically merged with health data. They are only used in shareable outputs when the user explicitly chooses to include them.

| Field | Rationale |
|-------|-----------|
| **Subject type** (Me / Someone I love) | **Problem:** The tool must know whether the data belongs to the user or another person to use the correct language and privacy handling. **Why needed:** Determines conditional routes and labelling. **Contributes to:** All outputs. **Required.** **When unknown:** Not applicable — user must choose. **Concerns:** Both. **Privacy:** No issue — it is a flag. **Burden avoided:** We do not assume the user is always the subject. |
| **Nickname or first name** (text, optional) | **Problem:** The user needs a way to refer to the subject without forcing a full legal name. **Why needed:** Personalises the interface and outputs (“Alex’s Heart Risk Audit”). **Contributes to:** All outputs if included. **Conditional** — only shown if subject is “Someone I love” or user wants to label their own profile. **When unknown:** Skip; use “your record” or “loved one”. **Concerns:** Both. **Privacy:** Separated; only shared if user opts in. **Burden avoided:** Not demanding full name reduces friction and privacy risk. |
| **Relationship to user** (dropdown: partner, parent, sibling, friend, etc., plus “prefer not to say”) | **Problem:** The user may need to communicate their role to a health professional. **Why needed:** Clarifies the helper’s relationship for the Health Team Discussion Brief. **Contributes to:** Discussion Brief, Take-Forward Pack. **Conditional** (only for someone I love). **When unknown:** “Prefer not to say”. **Concerns:** Someone I love. **Privacy:** Kept with identity; only shared if user opts in. **Burden avoided:** Not forcing a biological-family-centric list; “someone I love” covers chosen family. |
| **Year of birth** (not full date) | **Problem:** Age is relevant to heart risk context but full birth date is unnecessary and sensitive. **Why needed:** Allows outputs to mention age range for context (e.g., “in your 50s”). **Contributes to:** Audit Record, Discussion Brief. **Required.** **When unknown:** “I don’t know” — age will be omitted. **Concerns:** Both. **Privacy:** Only a year, separated. **Burden avoided:** Full date of birth would be excessive PII. |
| **Sex registered at birth** (optional, with clear note: “This helps contextualise some risk factors. It does not assume your gender identity.”) | **Problem:** Some heart risk factors are discussed differently in clinical settings based on sex. **Why needed:** Enables outputs to reference relevant population context without making assumptions. **Contributes to:** Audit Record (for context). **Conditional** — user can skip. **When unknown:** Skip. **Concerns:** Both. **Privacy:** Stored in health profile, not identity, because it is a health context field; user can choose to share. **Burden avoided:** Not asking for gender identity, and making the purpose clear. |

#### 5.2 Health and risk profile (non-identifying)
Stored separately from identity. Every health field is optional; the user can mark any field as “I don’t know” or skip.

##### 5.2.1 Known diagnoses and conditions
| Field | Rationale |
|-------|-----------|
| **Ever been told you have high blood pressure?** (Yes/No/Don’t know) | **Problem:** Many people are unaware whether a past reading was a one-off or a diagnosis. **Why needed:** Core modifiable risk factor. **Contributes to:** Audit Record, Missing-Info Summary. **Required.** **When unknown:** Marked as gap. **Concerns:** Subject. **Privacy:** Health data, separated. **Burden avoided:** Simple yes/no; not asking for numbers yet. |
| **Ever been told you have high cholesterol?** (Yes/No/Don’t know) | Similar rationale. |
| **Diagnosed with diabetes (type 1 or type 2)?** (Yes/No/Don’t know) | Similar. |
| **Diagnosed with a heart condition?** (Checklist: coronary artery disease, heart failure, arrhythmia, valve disease, congenital heart condition, other) | **Problem:** People may know the name of their condition but not whether it counts as “heart disease”. **Why needed:** Clarifies existing burden. **Contributes to:** Audit Record. **Required.** **When unknown:** “I’ve been told something but I’m not sure” -> free text. **Concerns:** Subject. **Privacy:** Health data. **Burden avoided:** Allowing free text for unclear diagnoses prevents people getting stuck. |
| **Ever had a heart attack, stroke or TIA?** (Yes/No/Don’t know) | Similar. |

##### 5.2.2 Measured values (when known)
Each numeric field allows “I don’t know” and includes an optional date.

| Field | Rationale |
|-------|-----------|
| **Most recent blood pressure reading** (systolic/diastolic, optional date) | **Problem:** People often have a rough number but no context. **Why needed:** Makes the Audit Record concrete. **Contributes to:** Audit Record, Discussion Brief. **Conditional** — only shown if user answers Yes/Don’t know to high BP. **When unknown:** Leave blank; the missing value becomes part of the Missing-Info Summary. **Concerns:** Subject. **Privacy:** Health data. **Burden avoided:** Not demanding a date if unknown. |
| **Most recent total cholesterol (if recalled)** and breakdown if available | Same pattern. |
| **HbA1c or fasting glucose** (if diabetes) | Same. |
| **Weight and height** (for BMI context, with a note that BMI is limited) | **Problem:** People may want to understand weight in context. **Why needed:** Captures a common discussion point without overinterpretation. **Contributes to:** Audit Record (as a note, not a risk calculation). **Conditional.** **When unknown:** Skip. **Concerns:** Subject. **Privacy:** Health data. **Burden avoided:** Not framing weight as a judgement; simply recording what they know. |

##### 5.2.3 Symptoms and experiences
| Field | Rationale |
|-------|-----------|
| **In the last 6 months, have you experienced…** (checklist: chest pain/tightness, shortness of breath, palpitations, unusual tiredness, dizziness/fainting, swelling in legs, other) with free text | **Problem:** People may not connect symptoms to heart health. **Why needed:** Provides conversation starters. **Contributes to:** Audit Record, Discussion Brief. **Conditional** — shown if no diagnosed condition, but available. **When unknown:** Can skip. **Concerns:** Subject. **Privacy:** Health data. **Burden avoided:** Not forcing a symptom diary; just a quick checklist. |
| **How would you describe your day-to-day energy and ability to do things you want?** (free text, optional) | **Problem:** Quality-of-life context often gets lost. **Why needed:** Gives health professional a patient-centred snapshot. **Contributes to:** Discussion Brief. **Conditional.** **When unknown:** Skip. **Concerns:** Subject. **Privacy:** Health data. **Burden avoided:** Open but optional. |

##### 5.2.4 Lifestyle factors
| Field | Rationale |
|-------|-----------|
| **Smoking** (never/former/current, with rough amounts if current) | **Problem:** Often asked but rarely prepared thoughtfully. **Why needed:** Major modifiable factor. **Contributes to:** Audit Record. **Required.** **When unknown:** “I’d rather not say” is allowed. **Concerns:** Subject. **Privacy:** Health data. **Burden avoided:** Not moralising; factual. |
| **Alcohol** (frequency pattern) | Similar. |
| **Physical activity** (brief description of typical week) | **Problem:** People under- or over-report. **Why needed:** Opens a non-judgemental conversation. **Contributes to:** Audit Record. **Conditional.** **When unknown:** Skip. **Privacy:** Health data. **Burden avoided:** No mandatory minutes-per-week calculator. |

##### 5.2.5 Family history
| Field | Rationale |
|-------|-----------|
| **Has a close biological family member had heart disease, heart attack or stroke, particularly at a younger age?** (Yes/No/Don’t know, with ability to note relation and age) | **Problem:** Family history is often vague in memory. **Why needed:** Strong inherited risk context. **Contributes to:** Audit Record, Missing-Info Summary. **Conditional** — the user can skip if family history is unknown or not relevant (e.g., adopted, estranged). **When unknown:** “I don’t know my family history” — captured as a gap. **Concerns:** Subject and biological relatives. **Privacy:** The identity of relatives is not asked for; only relationship type. **Burden avoided:** Not assuming everyone knows or has access to family history. We explicitly allow “I don’t know” without pressure. |

##### 5.2.6 Current medications
| Field | Rationale |
|-------|-----------|
| **Medications you take regularly** (free text, optional) | **Problem:** People forget names and doses. **Why needed:** Helps the health team avoid interactions and understand treatment. **Contributes to:** Discussion Brief. **Conditional.** **When unknown:** “I don’t have a list right now” — captured as an action to prepare. **Concerns:** Subject. **Privacy:** Health data. **Burden avoided:** Not requiring perfect recall; framing as “what you can remember now”. |

##### 5.2.7 User’s own questions and priorities
| Field | Rationale |
|-------|-----------|
| **What matters most to you right now?** (free text) | **Problem:** People may not be asked what they actually want. **Why needed:** Anchors the discussion brief in the person’s own words. **Contributes to:** Discussion Brief, Decision Record. **Required** at Stage 4, but can be filled at any time. **When unknown:** They can leave it until later. **Privacy:** Health data; deeply personal. **Burden avoided:** Not forcing a structured priority list; one open field. |
| **Questions you want to ask** (list with free text, can add more) | **Problem:** Questions are forgotten in the moment. **Why needed:** Turns anxiety into a practical list. **Contributes to:** Discussion Brief. **Conditional.** **When unknown:** Skippable; can add later. **Privacy:** Health data. |

#### 5.3 Missing-information gaps
When a user answers “I don’t know” to a field, or skips an important section, the system automatically notes it as a gap. The user can also manually add a gap (e.g., “I don’t understand what my cholesterol numbers mean”). Each gap includes a short description and is included in the Missing-Information Summary.

#### 5.4 Decisions and next actions record
After an appointment, or when the user is ready, they can enter:
- **What was decided today** (free text)
- **What I/we need to do next** (list with checkboxes)
- **Next appointment or review** (date, optional)
- **Things I want to remember** (free text)
These are stored with the profile and can be printed.

